<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese Restoration War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1640">1640</a>–<a href="/years/1668">1668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Acclamation War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portuguese Restoration War was fought from 1640 to 1668 after Portugal rebelled against Spanish rule and proclaimed John IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a> king, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a>. After long border warfare, Portuguese victories at Ameixial (1663), Castelo Rodrigo (1664) and Montes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claros">Claros</a> (1665) led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to recognise Portuguese independence in the Treaty of Lisbon (1668).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-restoration-war.jpg" alt="Portuguese Restoration War" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_der_vierte_K%C3%B6nig_zu_Portugal_und_Algarbe_etc,_ca_1650.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The succession crisis of 1580 created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a>, a personal union of Habsburg Spain and Portugal. After Philip IV succeeded his father in 1621, Portuguese merchants paid higher taxes, the nobility lost influence in the Cortes Generales, and appointments went increasingly to Spanish outsiders. Spanish involvement in the Eighty Years' War brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attacks on Portuguese colonies. With Spanish resources absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and a revolt in Catalonia, conspirators known as the Forty Conspirators struck on 1 December 1640. They killed the Secretary of State, Miguel de Vasconcelos, and imprisoned the Vicereine, Margaret of Savoy, and John, 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a>, was acclaimed King John IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war consisted mainly of border skirmishes and cavalry raids, with only five major battles in twenty-eight years. John IV created a Council of War on 11 December 1640 and sought alliances: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed on 1 June 1641, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> wanted Portuguese resistance to drain Spanish resources. France abandoned the alliance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659. Meanwhile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1641, and Portugal regained Angola in 1648, São Tomé in 1649 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1654.</p><p>The first stage (1640–1646) included the Portuguese victory at Montijo on 26 May 1644, and the second (1646–1660) was a standoff of small raids. In 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>, and an English brigade of about 3,000 arrived under Friedrich Hermann von Schönberg. In 1663 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria-the-younger">John of Austria the Younger</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, but the Portuguese defeated him at Ameixial on 8 June. After Castelo Rodrigo on 7 July 1664, the Spanish lost over 10,000 men at Montes Claros on 17 June 1665.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With English mediation, the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> of 1668 recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a> as Portugal's ruling dynasty. Independence freed Portugal to pursue its colonial trade, and after 1668 it cultivated intellectual ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The memory of Spanish invasion remained a strong support for Portuguese nationalism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portuguese Restoration War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
